WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
SPEAKER: Instead of developers
accessing your backend services

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:07.170
directly, they can be exposed
through API Proxies on Apigee,

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.780
which are HTTP endpoints.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.730
The proxy manages
any code changes

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:14.550
on the backend and avoids
disruptions for developers

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:18.040
until a new version of
the API is warranted.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:21.540
An API Proxy enables you
to apply security features

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.640
and force access
limits and quotas,

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.220
provide caching,
create transformations,

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.870
and handle errors.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.640
It also lets you
perform monitoring

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.000
and create analytic reports.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.910
You usually group multiple API
Proxies into an API product

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:38.290
and offer that as
a service plan.

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:41.100
For example, you may want
to provide an API product

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.940
for free up to a certain request
limit per day for developers

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.860
to try out your API.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.600
But we'll cover that
in another episode.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.220
You will have some flexibility
when creating your API Proxy.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.410
And so to get started,
in this episode,

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:59.522
I will walk you through how
to build your first API Proxy.

00:00:59.522 --> 00:01:03.370
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:05.080
Before getting
started, you will need

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.270
to have configured
access to your API

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.060
Proxy either externally
or internally.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:13.840
I have linked instructions in
this video called Accessing API

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.400
Proxies.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:19.450
Next, visit the Apigee
console at apigee.google.com.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.280
Then Develop API Proxies.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.680
Then select Create New.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.390
This will prompt
the proxy wizard,

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:30.870
and you will choose
Reverse Proxy.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.570
Next, enter the following
values for the proxy name,

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:38.190
proxy based path, description,
and the target existing API.

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:42.900
Then click Next, and Apigee
will create your proxy.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.540
Next, choose to visit
the API Proxy list

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.080
so that we can deploy
our proxy by returning

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:52.800
to Develop API Proxies again.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.590
Next, ensure you are on
your eval environment

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.100
by looking at the top.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.870
Otherwise, use the dropdown
to change environments.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.150
Click on My Proxy.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.580
Then notice that under
Deployments, the revision

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:08.460
column shows Not Deployed.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.110
Next, from the
revision dropdown,

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:13.890
choose one as this is the first
version and then click Deploy.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.100
After a few seconds pass, you
can see from the status bar

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.280
when your proxy
is fully deployed.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.880
Now, you can test the API
Proxy by using the command line

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.050
terminal.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:31.050
As a friendly reminder, if
you haven't configured access

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.430
to your proxies either
internally or externally,

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:36.630
I have linked instructions
on how to do that.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:38.970
Note that in my
case, the external IP

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:41.460
provided by an
external load balancer

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:45.060
is registered with a
DNS-registered hostname.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.270
Next, if the call succeeds,
you will see the Hello Guest

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.140
as the output.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:54.450
Now, let's try changing the
target endpoint to output XML.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:58.620
So let's return to
Develop, API proxies again,

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:00.870
select your desired proxy.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:03.120
Then on the top far
right, click Develop,

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.460
which will take you to
the API Proxy editor,

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.860
which lets you see the
structure of your API Proxy

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.450
and configure its flow.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:13.710
In the Navigator pane, select
Target Endpoints PreFlow

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:17.250
On the Code pane, you can
spot your target URL endpoint.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:20.460
Next, append forward slash
XML to the existing URL

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.440
so that it looks like this.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.060
Next, click Save.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.130
You'll be asked to save
it as a new revision

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.170
since you already
deployed revision one,

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.180
and that can't be changed.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.770
Then deploy it to
the new revision

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:38.080
by selecting the button Deploy
To Eval and choose Deploy To.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.000
Test your changes
by sending a request

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.250
to your proxy via
command line just

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:44.320
like we did earlier, like this.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:46.570
And this should return XML.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:50.080
Now, let's add a policy to
convert that XML into JSON.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.770
We will use an XML to JSON
policy in the proxy response

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:54.640
flow.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:59.080
This policy converts the payload
of an XML message to JSON.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:02.380
It also changes the response's
content type header.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.260
To add the XML to JSON
policy to your proxy, let's

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:09.290
return to API Proxies Develop.

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:10.760
Select your desired proxy.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:13.730
Then on the top far
right, click Develop.

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:19.310
This time, click Proxy,
Endpoints, Default, PreFlow.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:22.970
To add a policy to your proxy,
click the Plus Step button

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:27.290
in the response PreFlow, the
bottom half of the flow editor.

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:29.180
Apigee displays a
categorized list

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.610
of policies in
the Add dialog box

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.320
that you can add to your flow.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:37.640
Scroll down and select
the XML to JSON policy.

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:40.040
Leave the default
names and click Add.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:43.800
Apigee attaches the new policy
to the PreFlow of the response,

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:45.720
which does three things.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.890
One, it adds the new policy
under Policies in the Navigator

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:49.640
pane.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:52.970
Two, it adds the XML to JSON
policy in the flow pane.

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:55.880
And three, it displays the
policy's configuration XML

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:57.590
in the code pane.

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:00.290
We'll just use the default
configuration that's suggested.

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:03.440
But this can be customized.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:04.550
Next, click Save.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:07.950
And test it out with the
following co-command.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:08.760
Congrats.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.040
You've successfully
added the XML

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:14.130
to JSON policy to
your new proxy.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:17.160
As a next step, I have linked,
in this video's description,

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:20.490
more information on how to
add additional functionality

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:23.730
to your API proxies
if you're interested.

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:25.560
In the next episode,
I will walk you

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.720
through how to apply
Apigee security

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:29.550
policies to your proxy.

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.110
So stay tuned.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.390
And friends, if you found
this episode helpful,

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:36.390
you can click Like or
Subscribe to the channel.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:38.790
Cheers.

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:42.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

